he long side and the short side each add up to it, with spread positions (long one delivery month and short another) counted on both sides. These are bets on where US shares go, not holdings: an asset manager long these futures does not own what they are written on. Dealers and banks are mostly on the other side of their clients; asset managers include pension funds, insurers and mutual funds; leveraged funds are mainly hedge funds and trading advisers; small traders hold less than the CFTC's reporting size. Contracts of different futures are different sizes, so counts are compared within one book, never across books. Source: CFTC Commitments of Traders."}